    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": "Mental health of clinical staff working in high-risk epidemic and pandemic health emergencies a rapid review of the evidence and living meta-analysis",
      "reason": "The title explicitly identifies the report as a \"rapid review of the evidence,\" which is a type of systematic review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": "Mental health of clinical staff working in high-risk epidemic and pandemic health emergencies",
      "reason": "The title clearly states the population (\"clinical staff\") and the exposure (\"working in high-risk epidemic and pandemic health emergencies\") being investigated in relation to the outcome (\"Mental health\")."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": "a rapid review of the evidence and living meta-analysis",
      "reason": "The title provides supplementary information regarding the methodology (\"rapid review\") and the update status (\"living meta-analysis\")."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "The recent SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 pandemic has seen an increased demand on clinical staff... This has raised concerns about the potential impact on the mental health of epidemic and pandemic responders [1, 2]. Importantly however, high rates of poor mental health are common in clinical staff working in acute medicine generally [3-5] and so estimating the additional impact of epidemic and pandemic response, not solely the extent of poor mental health, is also important in guiding decisions to protect staff mental health.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge regarding the mental health of clinical staff during pandemics and identifies the uncertainty regarding the additional impact of pandemic response work compared to baseline mental health issues."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Consequently, we report a rapid review and meta-analysis that forms the basis of a living meta-analysis of the mental health of clinical staff dealing with epidemics and pandemics of high-risk infectious diseases... to understand the potential impact on mental health and to inform policy on supporting staff during the current COVID-19 pandemic.",
      "reason": "The authors clearly explain the importance and need for the review, specifically to understand the mental health impact and inform policy during the COVID-19 pandemic."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Recently, a meta-analysis by Kisely et al. [6] reported that healthcare workers in direct contact with affected patients had an approximately 1.71-1.74 odds ratio of reporting acute mental health problems... Given that the SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 pandemic is likely to evolve in location, course and severity over time, traditional meta-analyses are only likely to give a snapshot of the current state of the evidence. Living systematic reviews or meta-analyses are a new innovation that allow estimates to be updated as new evidence emerges [7, 8] but have rarely been used to date.",
      "reason": "The authors identify a similar existing systematic review (Kisely et al.) and explain the need for their current review (a living meta-analysis) to address the limitations of traditional meta-analyses in the context of an evolving pandemic."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is an observational review of the mental health impact of work, not a review of intervention effects."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is an observational review of the mental health impact of work, not a review of complex interventions requiring a logic model."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Consequently, we report a rapid review and meta-analysis that forms the basis of a living meta-analysis of the mental health of clinical staff dealing with epidemics and pandemics of high-risk infectious diseases... to understand the potential impact on mental health and to inform policy on supporting staff during the current COVID-19 pandemic.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly state the objective of the review: to understand the potential impact of high-risk infectious disease epidemics and pandemics on the mental health of clinical staff and to inform policy."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Numeric data from studies reporting (i) above cut-off prevalence or, (ii) means and standard deviations-from validated anxiety, post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) and depression symptom scales were extracted... a comparison of risk ratios for studies that compared above-cut off scoring between high- and low-exposure groups, and meta-analysis for differences in mean scale scores between high and low exposures",
      "reason": "The review compares high-exposure vs. low-exposure groups regarding mental health outcomes (anxiety, PTSD, depression), which constitutes a PICO-like structure (Population: clinical staff; Intervention/Exposure: high-exposure work; Comparison: low-exposure work; Outcome: mental health symptoms)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
